1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,098
Inkling 2 Mini: before, you'd stop and wait; your AI's written thinking cost you; now 12 layers loop 3 times, but how, why?

2
00:00:08,598 --> 00:00:10,696
Here's how, in one hard exam question.

3
00:00:11,196 --> 00:00:12,881
Before, an ordinary model,

4
00:00:12,881 --> 00:00:16,065
like Gemma or Qwen, ran each layer once per token.

5
00:00:16,365 --> 00:00:18,502
To think harder, it wrote its working out,

6
00:00:18,502 --> 00:00:20,142
line after line, and you waited.

7
00:00:20,642 --> 00:00:22,259
Now, since November 20,

8
00:00:22,259 --> 00:00:24,136
Thinking Machines' Inkling 2 Mini

9
00:00:24,136 --> 00:00:25,544
works it over in its head.

10
00:00:25,844 --> 00:00:28,958
Its middle 12 layers run 3 times, same weights:

11
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:31,684
48 layer steps from 24 layers,

12
00:00:31,684 --> 00:00:32,629
no bigger brain.

13
00:00:33,129 --> 00:00:34,252
So it can write less.

14
00:00:34,552 --> 00:00:36,609
In the maker's test on a research twin,

15
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:39,129
three passes wrote less than half the reasoning.

16
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:40,239
And it's open,

17
00:00:40,239 --> 00:00:42,939
about 20 gigabytes, for a 32 gig Mac.

18
00:00:43,439 --> 00:00:45,281
How? The middle layers loop.

19
00:00:45,581 --> 00:00:49,233
Why? More thinking per token, fewer tokens to wait for.

20
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:51,713
The catch: it still writes some reasoning,

21
00:00:51,713 --> 00:00:53,132
and loops add no new facts.

22
00:00:53,632 --> 00:00:56,063
Same exam; more working happens in its head.

23
00:00:56,563 --> 00:00:57,965
Subscribe for the next one.
